vendorPanelDetails.desiredOutcomes:
The Principal is seeking an architectural solution that builds upon the existing Federation-style concept design and delivers a practical, cost-effective and high-quality redevelopment that enhances the functionality and long-term operation of Pinjarra Park.

Key desired outcomes include:

- retaining and refining the preferred design intent while validating and completing the design for construction;

- delivering a coordinated design that can be constructed within the approved project budget;

- providing complete design and statutory approval documentation for both separable portions;

- minimising the overall racecourse shutdown period through efficient design, buildability and construction sequencing;

- supporting construction commencing following the major race meetings at the end of March 2027, with the racecourse operational again before December 2028;

- delivering a coordinated, compliant and fit-for-purpose design that supports the future growth and long-term sustainability of the Pinjarra Race Club.

vendorPanelDetails.background:
Cassowary Coast Regional Council has received funding under the Queensland Government’s Scheme Supply Fund, to deliver a major amendment to the Cassowary Coast Planning Scheme 2015 to address housing supply. The funding also presents an opportunity to review the planning scheme more broadly, to:

•	Review and set the strategic direction for the Cassowary Coast Region for the next 10 years;

•	Consider whether the current planning scheme is delivering development and infrastructure outcomes, which are consistent with current community, industry and Council expectations;

•	Discuss and consider alternative approaches and outcomes to address those areas that have not delivered;

•	Deliver a more proactive approach to plan making, which provides greater specificity for preferred planning outcomes for key sectoral opportunities for additional housing within our region is considered as essential to attract realistic and sustainable investment opportunities and meet the region’s growth needs; and

•	Review planning scheme provision in context of the regions location and emerging development trends being experienced across regional Australia.

publicQuestions.question:
Previous housing engagement

Has Council undertaken any recent community, stakeholder or industry engagement specifically relating to housing needs, housing supply, housing diversity, affordability or barriers to residential development within the Cassowary Coast Region? If so, could Council please identify the relevant engagement activities and advise whether the findings, consultation records or reports will be made available to the successful consultant?

Existing stakeholder relationships

Does Council maintain an existing stakeholder database, reference group or established relationships with organisations that are expected to be engaged through this project, including community organisations, development industry representatives, housing and homelessness service providers, community housing providers, major employers and relevant State Government agencies? If so, will Council facilitate introductions and/or provide appropriate stakeholder contact information to the successful consultant?

First Nations engagement protocols

Does Council have established relationships, engagement protocols or existing consultation arrangements with Traditional Owners, First Nations organisations, Prescribed Bodies Corporate, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community organisations or other relevant First Nations representatives within the Cassowary Coast Region that should be followed for this project?

First Nations engagement responsibilities

Given the requirement for the Planning Scheme amendment to appropriately recognise and advance Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander knowledge, culture and tradition, could Council clarify whether it expects Council to lead engagement with First Nations stakeholders through its existing relationships and protocols, or whether the successful Study Team will be responsible for establishing and managing these consultation processes?

Consultation responsibility matrix

Could Council please clarify the anticipated division of responsibilities between Council and the successful Study Team for the consultation and engagement components of the project? In particular, it would be helpful to understand who is expected to be responsible for:

- stakeholder identification and invitations;

- provision and management of stakeholder contact details;

- booking venues and coordinating consultation logistics;

- preparation of engagement materials;

- facilitation of workshops, meetings and community sessions;

- online engagement and survey administration;

- engagement with elected representatives;

- engagement with State Government agencies;

- First Nations engagement;

- recording and analysis of consultation outcomes; and

- preparation of consultation reports and incorporation of findings into the Housing Needs Assessment, Housing Strategy and Planning Scheme amendment.

Extent of preliminary consultation already completed

The scope identifies preliminary community consultation as an early step in the Planning Scheme amendment process, prior to preparation of the supporting studies and proposed amendment. Has any component of this preliminary consultation already commenced or been completed, and if so, what additional consultation is expected to be undertaken by the successful Study Team?

Council participation in consultation

What level of Council officer and elected-member participation should tenderers assume for stakeholder workshops, community sessions and other engagement activities? For example, should tenderers assume Council representatives will attend and participate in all major engagement activities, or should the Study Team allow for independent facilitation of some sessions?

